1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,971
Bengal's Amavasya night glows differently.

2
00:00:03,921 --> 00:00:13,342
While much of India celebrates Diwali, West Bengal erupts for Kali Puja — the night the Dark Mother walks among us.

3
00:00:13,342 --> 00:00:23,421
In temples along the Hooghly, she arrives as Bama Kali: serene, bridal, blessing devotees with peace and sweet offerings.

4
00:00:23,421 --> 00:00:26,921
But Ma is never only one story.

5
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:35,960
At the cremation ground — the shashan — Shashan Kali reigns: terrible, compassionate, destroying ego so truth can breathe.

6
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:46,368
In North Kolkata's lanes, Phatakeshto Kali rides youth and rebellion — bold, street-side, fiercely loved at midnight feasts.

7
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:49,500
Chamunda rides the war-torn field.

8
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,131
Siddheshwari whispers in old North Calcutta homes.

9
00:00:54,131 --> 00:00:58,921
At Adyapeeth, Adya Ma holds centuries of tantra.

10
00:00:58,921 --> 00:01:02,434
Rakta Kali marks triumph over evil.

11
00:01:02,434 --> 00:01:06,342
Every para builds her from bamboo and clay.

12
00:01:06,342 --> 00:01:11,078
Artisans paint the third eye until she seems to blink.

13
00:01:11,078 --> 00:01:18,407
Then the night breaks open — dhak drums, conch shells, lamps, sindoor in the air.

14
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:20,342
Different names.

15
00:01:20,342 --> 00:01:22,355
Different altars.

16
00:01:22,355 --> 00:01:24,184
One Bengal.

17
00:01:24,184 --> 00:01:25,921
One Mother.

18
00:01:25,921 --> 00:01:27,842
Jay Ma Kali!

